            "relevance_eFtfkY": "- **Relevance Score:** 63/100\n- **Relevance Tag:** Somewhat Relevant\n- **Reasoning:** Uses multilevel data to quantify inmate\u2011on\u2011staff assaults, highlighting power conflict and aggression. Mentions prison demographics and staffing ratios, but lacks detailed environment description or officer stress analysis. Provides policy suggestions without deep legal analysis.",
            "abstract_mEOOqY": "Most of the extant literature on prison violence has explored inmate-on-inmate assaultive behaviors rather than inmate-on-staff assaults. In addition, the bulk of this past literature considered only one level of an analysis, the inmate or the prison, while ignoring the importance of prison context on inmate behavior. This study enhances past research by combining both inmate- and prison-level data into a multilevel model predicting the likelihood of inmate-on-staff assaults. Self-report data from more than 1,000 inmates and 30 prisons revealed that, at the inmate level, age and aggression were the most robust predictors of inmate-on-staff assaults. In terms of contextual effects, inmates housed in prisons with a greater proportion of non-White inmates and a larger staff-to-inmate ratio were more likely to assault prison staff members. Policy implications and suggestions for future research are discussed.",
            "gang-dynamics_Y8XYpP": "Not available in metadata.",
            "prison-life-a_IPKKQX": "The study examined inmate-on-staff assaults, finding that age and aggression were key inmate-level predictors. Contextual factors included a higher likelihood of assaults in prisons with a greater proportion of non-White inmates and a larger staff-to-inmate ratio. The research combined inmate- and prison-level data from over 1,000 inmates and 30 prisons to understand these dynamics.",
            "prison-condit_y7RVPo": "Not available in metadata.",
            "violence-and_Td4kKP": "This study used self-report data from more than 1,000 inmates and 30 prisons. At the inmate level, age and aggression were the most robust predictors of inmate-on-staff assaults. Contextually, inmates in prisons with a greater proportion of non-White inmates and a larger staff-to-inmate ratio were more likely to assault prison staff members. The research focused on inmate-on-staff assaults, distinguishing it from the more common inmate-on-inmate violence literature.",
            "staff-prisone_8CiEa7": "The study primarily focuses on inmate-on-staff assaults and their predictors, rather than the broader nature of staff-prisoner relationship dynamics. It identifies that inmates in prisons with a larger staff-to-inmate ratio were more likely to assault staff members. The research combines inmate- and prison-level data to predict assault likelihood, with inmate age and aggression being key individual predictors. The metadata does not delve into communication patterns, trust, respect, power dynamics, or staff attitudes beyond the context of assault likelihood.",
            "prison-life-p_DJkWS8": "Not available in metadata.",
            "gang-dynamics_xfeOqx": "The study examined inmate-on-staff assaults in prisons, finding that age and aggression were key inmate-level predictors. Contextual effects showed inmates in prisons with a greater proportion of non-White inmates and a larger staff-to-inmate ratio were more likely to assault staff. Data from over 1,000 inmates and 30 prisons were used in this multilevel model.",
            "correctional_j9SRjl": "The study examined inmate-on-staff assaults in 30 prisons, using data from over 1,000 inmates. It found that a larger staff-to-inmate ratio in prisons was associated with an increased likelihood of inmates assaulting staff members. The research combined inmate- and prison-level data to predict these assaults."
        },
        {
            "papers_MKAHdn": {
                "id": 1925733622,
                "title": "The late-modern American jail: epistemologies of space and violence",
                "is_archived": false,
                "full_slug": "the-late-modern-american-jail-epistemologies-of-space-and-50vgb41kck",
                "journal": {
                    "display_name": "The Geographical Journal",
                    "official_page": "http://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/journal/10.1111/(ISSN)1475-4959",
                    "issn": "0016-7398",
                    "alias": [
                        "Geographical Journal",
                        "Geogr. J."
                    ],
                    "id": 54255202,
                    "slug": "the-geographical-journal",
                    "unique_id": "8zok5zh0",
                    "full_slug": "the-geographical-journal-8zok5zh0"
                },
                "conference_series": null,
                "date": "2016-03-01 00:00:00+00:00",
                "is_oa": false,
                "publication_type": "Journal Article",
                "doi": "10.1111/GEOJ.12121",
                "unique_id": "50vgb41kck",
                "abstract": "One of the most troubling aspects of current trends in American mass incarceration is the extent to which \u2018criminality\u2019 is produced within prison walls, primarily in the form of inmate\u2013inmate or inmate\u2013staff assaults. Most methods of prison or jail control have the adverse, and perverse, effect of increasing inmates' levels of fear, terror, and ultimately violence \u2013 with stabbings, beatings, and other types of assaults common occurrences. The design of podular \u2018direct supervision\u2019 jails and their accompanying philosophies of punishment aspire to change these conditions. Direct supervision features correctional officers inside each housing unit with no physical barriers impeding supervision, and is intended to create a safe, more humane, stress-free environment for both inmates and staff. This study draws on ethnographic evidence of inmate experiences with direct supervision at Douglas County Department of Corrections in Omaha, Nebraska (USA). The respondents generally do feel safe in this jail environment, for a combination of reasons, some of which are related to spatial design. Inmates identified a number of implications \u2013 gains and losses \u2013 of this more \u2018humane\u2019 form of incarceration in terms of power and empowerment. The study also documents the potential for inmate activism on their own behalf through this design.",
                "authors": {
