            "relevance_eFtfkY": "- **Relevance Score:** 62/100\n- **Relevance Tag:** Somewhat Relevant\n- **Reasoning:** Uses spatial design analysis to link environment perception with violence; provides self\u2011reported assault rates versus official data; mentions inmate\u2011staff conflict but lacks guard stress or program details.",
            "abstract_mEOOqY": "One of the most troubling aspects of current trends in American mass incarceration is the extent to which \u2018criminality\u2019 is produced within prison walls, primarily in the form of inmate\u2013inmate or inmate\u2013staff assaults. Most methods of prison or jail control have the adverse, and perverse, effect of increasing inmates' levels of fear, terror, and ultimately violence \u2013 with stabbings, beatings, and other types of assaults common occurrences. The design of podular \u2018direct supervision\u2019 jails and their accompanying philosophies of punishment aspire to change these conditions. Direct supervision features correctional officers inside each housing unit with no physical barriers impeding supervision, and is intended to create a safe, more humane, stress-free environment for both inmates and staff. This study draws on ethnographic evidence of inmate experiences with direct supervision at Douglas County Department of Corrections in Omaha, Nebraska (USA). The respondents generally do feel safe in this jail environment, for a combination of reasons, some of which are related to spatial design. Inmates identified a number of implications \u2013 gains and losses \u2013 of this more \u2018humane\u2019 form of incarceration in terms of power and empowerment. The study also documents the potential for inmate activism on their own behalf through this design.",
            "gang-dynamics_Y8XYpP": "The Douglas County Department of Corrections (DCDC) groups inmates by risk classification, including gang affiliation, using the NIC Model of Classification. Known members of rival gangs are not housed together, a practice termed 'keep separate.' This policy aims to prevent inter-gang conflicts and manage security threat groups. Gang culture pervades correctional facilities, influencing inmate interactions and potentially impacting prison operations and security.",
            "prison-life-a_IPKKQX": "Inmates in direct supervision jails spend days in day rooms, locked in cells at night. They can circulate and interact with correctional officers (COs), which some found improved safety and security. Open, interactive spaces allowed sharing information and life experiences, including discussions about basic rights, racial justice, and the American justice system. However, lack of privacy in bathrooms/showers and the open unit layout were noted as challenges.",
            "prison-condit_y7RVPo": "Inmates at DCDC spend days in the day room and are locked into cells at night. Living conditions include cells for sleeping, dayroom space with tables/seats, hygiene facilities, food service areas, and recreation yards. The facility has 30 housing units, 26 for men and four for women, with the largest housing 64 inmates. Visits, except for chaplains and attorneys, are conducted through video monitors. Inmates commented on poor food quality and lack of privacy in bathrooms and showers.",
            "violence-and_Td4kKP": "In the DCDC, 23% of respondents reported involvement in a physical conflict since incarceration. Official counts for 2011-12 showed 92 inmate-on-inmate assaults (4 with weapons, 57 without) and 31 inmate-on-staff assaults (5 with weapons, 26 without). Medical injuries sustained were 21 in 2011-12, 135 in 2010-11, 156 in 2009-10, and 141 in 2008-09. 'Forced moves' occurred 39 times in 2011-12. Direct supervision reduced violent incidents by 30% to 90% in other studies.",
            "staff-prisone_8CiEa7": "*   **Communication Patterns:** In direct supervision jails, inmates report better help and communication with COs due to their presence in the room, enabling face-to-face interaction. However, one-on-one conversations can be perceived as \"snitching.\"\n*   **Trust Levels & Safety:** Some inmates feel safer because COs are present and watching, reducing conflict. Others feel less safe due to the open unit layout making them vulnerable, or because some COs are strict while others are lenient.\n*   **Power Dynamics:** The layout can give COs easy opportunity to take action against inmates or invade privacy, diminishing feelings of safety for some.\n*   **Staff Attitudes/Behaviors:** Direct supervision staff are less bored, know inmates better, and tend to overreact less often.",
            "prison-life-p_DJkWS8": "Inmates in US direct supervision jails spend days in dayrooms, locked in cells at night. They have access to hygiene facilities, food service areas, and recreation yards. Visits are typically conducted through video monitors, except for chaplains and attorneys. Inmates discuss topics like family, news, and self-help. Concerns include lack of privacy in bathrooms/showers and poor food quality.",
            "gang-dynamics_xfeOqx": "Active gangs, including white, African-American, and Latino, are present in the Douglas County Department of Corrections (DCDC), where a \"keep separate\" policy is used for rival gang members. Inmate-on-inmate and inmate-on-staff assaults are common occurrences in US prisons. DCDC incident summaries (2008-12) show annual inmate-on-inmate assaults with/without weapons ranging from 54 to 83, and inmate-on-staff assaults from 17 to 75. Some inmates also reported physical conflicts (23%).",
            "correctional_j9SRjl": "In U.S. direct supervision facilities, correctional officers (COs) work inside housing units without physical barriers, fostering interaction with inmates. Training requirements for new COs can be five weeks of classroom training plus four weeks of field training, though two-thirds of staff surveyed in one study received less than 12 weeks of training. Staff job satisfaction and on-the-job practices are important for direct supervision success. COs in direct supervision jails are less bored and overreact less often due to constant inmate interaction."
        },
