            "relevance_eFtfkY": "- **Relevance Score:** 58/100\n- **Relevance Tag:** Somewhat Relevant\n- **Reasoning:** Analyzes guard power, retaliation, and grievance processes; touches on prison bureaucracy and officer stress; lacks violence data; no rehab programs; cites historic studies.",
            "abstract_mEOOqY": "",
            "gang-dynamics_Y8XYpP": "Not addressed in this paper.",
            "prison-life-a_IPKKQX": "Inmates' daily life in prison is characterized by round-the-clock surveillance and regulation of nearly every aspect of their existence within a \"total institution.\" This environment generates abundant surplus power for correctional officers. Inmates face an adversarial relationship with prison staff, often experiencing fabricated disciplinary charges, transfers to less desirable cells, loss of jobs, or even assault and harassment. A distinct inmate subculture exists, with an unwritten code that includes condemning \"snitches\" and fostering solidarity among inmates as a means of coping with deprivations.",
            "prison-condit_y7RVPo": "Prison life is characterized by inmates being stripped of autonomy and subjected to round-the-clock surveillance within a \"total institution\" where all aspects of life are under single authority. Daily activities are tightly scheduled, and inmates are subject to a plethora of rules, many of which have no criminal law counterpart. Inmates may work full-time in various assignments, including office furniture production, call centers, or manufacturing. Living conditions can include exposure to cellmate's cigarette smoke and the physical environment can be harsh.",
            "violence-and_Td4kKP": "The PDF states that 70.1% of inmates who grieved suffered retaliation, and 87% of all respondents experienced retaliation (p. 4). A New York State study found over half of inmates filing grievances reported subsequent retaliation (p. 5). Disciplinary hearings result in a 90% conviction rate (p. 7). The PLRA led to a 60% drop in inmate filings per 1,000 inmates (p. 34). One case involved 60 days of solitary confinement for a First Amendment rights claim (p. 26).",
            "staff-prisone_8CiEa7": "Retaliation is deeply ingrained in the correctional subculture, often the normative response to inmate grievances. Correctional officers possess \"surplus power,\" leading to an adversarial relationship. Staff attitudes often view inmates as \"moral inferiors,\" and the inmate code condemns \"snitches.\" Correctional officers' wide discretion and vague rules mask retaliatory intent, with inmates often viewing disciplinary hearings as \"kangaroo courts.\" This dynamic undermines trust, leading to fear of retaliation among inmates.",
            "prison-life-p_DJkWS8": "Inmates in U.S. prisons live in \"total institutions\" where all aspects of existence are controlled by a single governmental authority, including access to health care, food, and exercise. Prison rules regulate nearly every daily activity, from bodily security to movement and property. Many inmates work full-time in various roles, from manufacturing to administrative duties, which can include making office furniture or stamping license plates. Historically, courts have recognized limited rights for inmates, including access to courts and some First Amendment protections.",
            "gang-dynamics_xfeOqx": "In U.S. prisons, an inmate culture portrays inmates and correctional officers as adversaries, with an inmate code condemning \"snitches\" who are \"hated [and] despised,\" and subject to brutal treatment. This code contributes to a pervasive unwritten code of silence, resulting in most acts of violence going undetected. Correctional officers also operate under a code that includes \"don't rat\" and \"support officer sanctions against inmates.\" The relationship between officers and inmates is often fraught with tension and hostility.",
            "correctional_j9SRjl": "Correctional officers possess \"surplus power\" over inmates, operating within a subculture that often views inmates as \"moral inferiors.\" Officer-prisoner relationships are characterized by \"structured conflict\" and tension. Training is extensive and professionalized due to complex administrative regulations. The officer code includes norms like \"don't rat\" and \"maintain officer solidarity.\" Retaliation by staff is deeply engrained, with a survey finding 70.1% of inmates experienced it after grievances. Specific statistics on staffing levels, salaries, working conditions, stress factors, or turnover rates are not provided."
        },
        {
            "papers_MKAHdn": {
                "id": 3208396018,
                "title": "Impacts of professionalization and wellbeing policies on Scottish prison workers",
                "is_archived": false,
                "full_slug": "impacts-of-professionalization-and-wellbeing-policies-on-1gx5tl9ygm",
                "journal": {
                    "display_name": "Frontiers in Sociology",
                    "official_page": "http://journal.frontiersin.org/journal/sociology",
                    "issn": "2297-7775",
                    "alias": null,
                    "id": 2596068225,
                    "slug": "frontiers-in-sociology",
                    "unique_id": "1vk4718e",
                    "full_slug": "frontiers-in-sociology-1vk4718e"
                },
                "conference_series": null,
                "date": "2021-10-25 00:00:00+00:00",
                "is_oa": true,
                "publication_type": "Journal Article",
                "doi": "10.3389/FSOC.2021.757583",
                "unique_id": "1gx5tl9ygm",
                "abstract": "Prison workers occupy a niche role. Balancing the care and welfare of prisoners while simultaneously restricting their freedoms is a stressful job, laced with danger, that occurs entirely within the bounded context of the prison. Here, wellbeing and professionalism are closely linked and articulated through a range of policies. This article explores the perceptions and experiences of staff in relation to a range of wellbeing and training policies in the Scottish Prison Service (SPS). We interviewed 10 SPS employees, some working directly with prisoners and others in more centralised policy development and support roles. Thematic analysis found a high degree of contentment with such policies but highlighted tensions between their implementation and specific challenges of the prison context. Emerging themes included: supporting wellbeing within the complex dynamic of the prison world; addressing inherent tensions borne out of the underlying threat of violence; and the impact of professionalization. We conclude that while the prison service aspires to offer employees wellbeing and professionalization opportunities similar to those in other sectors, there is a need for such policies to more clearly reflect the unique context of prison work. This might involve co-design of policies and more careful consideration of the pressures, tensions and idiosyncrasies of that rarefied environment.",
                "authors": {
                    "total": 4,
