            "relevance_eFtfkY": "- **Relevance Score:** 53/100\n- **Relevance Tag:** Somewhat Relevant\n- **Reasoning:** Uses ethnography to link officer stress and mental\u2011health narratives to daily interactions with inmates; offers limited insight on rights violations and policy implications, but lacks environmental or violence data.",
            "abstract_mEOOqY": "Correctional officers occupy key positions of power and influence in prisons, yet experience massive stress and perceive themselves as vulnerable. Existing research outlines the significant mental health challenges officers face, but there is limited information on exactly how mental health concerns influence officer behavior on a day-to-day basis. I draw on ethnographic observations and semi-structured interviews with 131 Canadian provincial correctional officers, to demonstrate that stress, perceptions of vulnerability, and tension between management and staff strongly influence officer behavior. My participants outline common officer narratives relating to mental health, posttraumatic stress disorder (PTSD), and even suicide. In addition, a portion of officers describe using substances to manage stress. I detail how these narratives influence officer behavior toward prisoners, managers, other officers, and people outside prison. I conclude by examining existing mental health programming for officers and assess what steps correctional administrators can take to address common concerns.",
            "gang-dynamics_Y8XYpP": "Not available in metadata.",
            "prison-life-a_IPKKQX": "The study focuses on correctional officers, detailing how their stress, vulnerability, and management tensions influence their behavior. It reveals common officer narratives about mental health, PTSD, and suicide, and some officers' substance use to manage stress. This impacts officer behavior toward prisoners, managers, other officers, and people outside prison. The paper examines existing mental health programming for officers and suggests administrative steps. Information on daily life in prison for inmates, including their routines, living conditions, experiences, or social dynamics, is not available in the metadata.",
            "prison-condit_y7RVPo": "Not available in metadata.",
            "violence-and_Td4kKP": "Not available in metadata.",
            "staff-prisone_8CiEa7": "The study reveals that stress, vulnerability, and management tensions strongly influence correctional officer behavior, impacting interactions with prisoners. Officers share narratives of mental health struggles, PTSD, and substance use to manage stress. These factors influence officer behavior toward prisoners, managers, and other officers. The research details how these narratives and stressors affect officer conduct, highlighting the complex dynamics within the prison environment.",
            "prison-life-p_DJkWS8": "Not available in metadata.",
