            "relevance_eFtfkY": "- **Relevance Score:** 52/100\n- **Relevance Tag:** Somewhat Relevant\n- **Reasoning:** Uses inmate\u2011staff interview data to detail supportive vs abusive guard interactions and sexual assault cases, providing qualitative conflict counts; mentions limited prison routine details; lacks officer stress, rehab programs, policy analysis, or cross\u2011system comparisons.",
            "abstract_mEOOqY": "",
            "gang-dynamics_Y8XYpP": "In prison, co-inmates formed cliques, and interaction was often for protection, with some men describing a \"kill or get killed\" environment. Gang membership was a factor in physical altercations among men, a phenomenon not reported by women. One respondent noted that \"everybody is governed by somebody\" inside prison, implying a structured environment.",
            "prison-life-a_IPKKQX": "During incarceration, men and women in the study experienced shifts in their social ties. For men, prison life was characterized by a \"kill or get killed\" mentality, with a focus on survival and the formation of cliques for protection. Women reported interacting with co-inmates for companionship and rarely experienced physical altercations. Both men and women had contact with prison staff and formed ministerial ties, with women generally experiencing more positive relationships with newly-formed weak-ties than men.",
            "prison-condit_y7RVPo": "The PDF mentions that during incarceration, men described prison as a \"playground\" or a \"world inside of a world\" where one had to \"program your mind to function, to survive.\" One inmate noted \"more guns and drugs inside prison\" and that \"you never feel completely safe nowhere.\" Work in prison included various types with compensation. Contact with intimate partners was through in-person visits, mail, and telephone. Prison staff relationships varied from supportive to problematic, with one woman reporting sexual assault by a guard.",
            "violence-and_Td4kKP": "The study noted that female inmates reported more extensive histories of physical and sexual abuse than male inmates (p. 18). During incarceration, two women (8%) engaged in physical altercations with co-inmates, limited to one incident each (p. 96). Four men (16%) described difficulties in co-inmate relationships, resulting in physical violence, with two men linking this to gang membership. Three of these men reported multiple altercations (p. 107). One woman (W4) reported continuous sexual assault by a male prison guard (p. 108). Seven men (28%) described at least some negative relationships with prison staff, with one man attempting to kill a guard (p. 108-109).",
            "staff-prisone_8CiEa7": "- **Women's Relationships with Prison Staff:**\n    - 32% found relationships good, supportive, and encouraging.\n    - 60% cited lack of support and abuse of authority.\n    - 8% labeled relationships explicitly negative, including sexual assault by an officer.\n\n- **Men's Relationships with Prison Staff:**\n    - 64% reported problem-free and respectful relationships.\n    - 28% described some relationships as negative, with initial problems due to anger over incarceration.\n    - 12% had nothing positive to say, citing racism or staff reluctance to help.\n\n- **Gender Influence:** Women focused on emotional support, men on instrumental support from parole officers.",
            "prison-life-p_DJkWS8": "Prison life in the U.S. involves a range of daily experiences. Many returning offenders live with families post-release, but temporary housing options can be dangerous and conducive to drug/alcohol use or criminal offending. In prison, inmates often rely on family for financial support. Contact with children is maintained through visits, letters, and phone calls. Work programs and educational training are available, though women are less likely to participate due to shorter sentences. Prison staff interactions can be supportive or negative.",
            "gang-dynamics_xfeOqx": "In U.S. prisons, gang membership among inmates is a factor in physical violence. For men, physical altercations with co-inmates were sometimes due to gang membership, a phenomenon not reported by women. Violence against others by men in prison may reflect masculine ideals or power loss. The study noted men's relationships with co-inmates were sometimes for protection, forming \"cliques\" that \"would kill for one another.\"",
            "correctional_j9SRjl": "The provided document discusses officer-prisoner relationships, noting that some women found parole officers supportive, while others felt they were too busy to form close relationships. Men generally found parole officers helpful and concerned with their success, though some had negative experiences with previous officers. The document mentions that prison staff can be a source of emotional support and that parole officers are a primary source of information about parole resources. However, it does not provide specific statistics on employment structure, staffing levels, salaries, working conditions, stress factors, training requirements, or turnover rates for U.S. correctional staff."
        },
        {
            "papers_MKAHdn": {
                "unique_id": "pni7vlczlq",
                "id": 1972100112,
                "full_slug": "the-influence-of-prison-staff-on-inmate-conditions-a-pni7vlczlq",
                "title": "The influence of prison staff on inmate conditions: A multilevel approach to staff and inmate surveys",
                "abstract": "The current study connects survey data of inmates and correctional staff in the Dutch prison system in order to describe and explain the impact of staff orientation and staff working conditions on perceived prison circumstances of inmates. Importation and deprivation theory are combined to test an integrated model to explain perceived prison conditions. By surveying staff (N = 1750) and inmates (N = 4673) independently within the same period of time and by afterwards pairing the results on the level of the housing unit (N = 173) using multilevel techniques, it is found that inmates\u00b4 perceptions of the prison conditions vary considerably between housing units. It is also found that staff\u2019s perceptions of prison conditions show congruency with those of inmates. Another important finding is that in housing units where the orientation of staff towards inmates is relatively supportive, inmates perceive their circumstances as more positive. Conclusions and directions for further research are provided.",
                "is_archived": false,
                "publication_type": "Journal Article",
                "doi": "10.1007/S10610-011-9158-7",
                "date": "2012-06-01 00:00:00+00:00",
