            "abstract_mEOOqY": "The current study connects survey data of inmates and correctional staff in the Dutch prison system in order to describe and explain the impact of staff orientation and staff working conditions on perceived prison circumstances of inmates. Importation and deprivation theory are combined to test an integrated model to explain perceived prison conditions. By surveying staff (N = 1750) and inmates (N = 4673) independently within the same period of time and by afterwards pairing the results on the level of the housing unit (N = 173) using multilevel techniques, it is found that inmates\u00b4 perceptions of the prison conditions vary considerably between housing units. It is also found that staff\u2019s perceptions of prison conditions show congruency with those of inmates. Another important finding is that in housing units where the orientation of staff towards inmates is relatively supportive, inmates perceive their circumstances as more positive. Conclusions and directions for further research are provided.",
            "gang-dynamics_Y8XYpP": "Not available in metadata.",
            "prison-life-a_IPKKQX": "The study found that inmates' perceptions of prison conditions vary considerably between housing units. Staff's perceptions of prison conditions show congruency with those of inmates. In housing units where staff orientation towards inmates is relatively supportive, inmates perceive their circumstances as more positive. The research connects staff orientation and working conditions to perceived prison circumstances of inmates, combining importation and deprivation theory to explain these conditions.",
            "prison-condit_y7RVPo": "The study connects survey data of inmates and correctional staff in the Dutch prison system to explain the impact of staff orientation and working conditions on perceived prison circumstances of inmates. It found inmates' perceptions of prison conditions vary considerably between housing units. In units where staff orientation was relatively supportive, inmates perceived their circumstances as more positive. The research focuses on perceived prison conditions and staff orientation.",
