                "title": "Security, Harmony, and \u2018What Matters\u2019 in Prison Life",
                "is_archived": false,
                "full_slug": "security-harmony-and-what-matters-in-prison-life-30p3m6ffgt",
                "journal": null,
                "conference_series": null,
                "date": "2004-05-27 00:00:00+00:00",
                "is_oa": null,
                "publication_type": "Book Chapter",
                "doi": "10.1093/oso/9780199271221.003.0009",
                "unique_id": "30p3m6ffgt",
                "abstract": "Abstract So far we have reported our results rather evenly and, perhaps, uncritically. There are important questions still to be addressed, one of which we shall consider in this chapter. This question relates to a distinction we have drawn in previous work, and which emerged towards the end of our research, between \u2018good\u2019 and \u2018right\u2019 relationships (Liebling and Price 2001: 75\u201384), or between a theory of the moral and a theory of \u2018being nice\u2019 (McEvoy 2003). \u2018Good\u2019 relationships can be flexible, tolerant, harmonious, and respectful. They can also be overly lenient, sometimes resulting in inconsistency, perceptions of unfairness, and a lack of safety, order, and security in prison, as we suggested in Chapter 1. Staff can get too close to prisoners, identifying with their needs and concerns to the exclusion of other considerations. ",
                "authors": {
                    "total": 2,
                    "data": [
                        {
                            "display_name": "Alison Liebling",
                            "full_slug": null
                        },
                        {
                            "display_name": "Helen Arnold",
                            "full_slug": null
                        }
                    ]
                },
                "affiliations": {
